-- Pass 100 §22 — the capability matrix, PER CAPABILITY.
--
-- The two reports next door answer population questions.
-- scripts/capability_matrix.duo asks "how far does the corpus get" and
-- scripts/capability_table.duo asks "how far does each FIXTURE get". Neither
-- answers the question §22 is actually asked in review, which is "does THIS
-- capability work, and how far along the pipeline". A per-file table cannot
-- answer it, because one capability is demonstrated by several files and one
-- file demonstrates several capabilities.
--
-- So the rows here are CAPABILITIES and the columns are the pipeline of §22:
--
--     lexical compatibility -> one canonical syntax graph -> one semantic graph
--     -> relations/trie/worlds/demand/witnesses -> derived phase views
--     -> representation choice -> native execution
--
--     D  described    the fixture states its intent: its FIRST line is a `--`
--                     comment. A program with no statement of intent is a
--                     program, not evidence. First line, not any line, so a
--                     `-- x` buried at line 40 does not earn the rung.
--     P  parsed       `duo fmt` exits 0 on a COPY of the fixture. fmt needs a
--                     parse and nothing after it, which is the cleanest split
--                     between the front end and sema this CLI offers — `duo
--                     check` reports parse and sema failures through one
--                     channel. ON A COPY because fmt REWRITES ITS ARGUMENT IN
--                     PLACE and drops comments (gap[048]); a measurement must
--                     not be able to write to what it measures.
--     S  semantic     `duo check <f>` exits 0.
--     G  graph        `duo graph <f>` exits 0, emits JSON, and that JSON
--                     CONTAINS THE ROW'S DECLARED GRAPH KEY — the schema
--                     string that would have to be present for the exported
--                     semantic graph to be said to represent this capability.
--                     The key is PRINTED with the row so the reader can judge
--                     it. A row whose key is `-` declares that the graph
--                     schema has NO slot for this capability at all, and its
--                     cell is `?`, never a `✓`. See the G NOTES section: the
--                     JSON carries no `edges` array for any fixture, so the
--                     relation half of this pipeline stage is unexported
--                     repo-wide and no row can honestly claim it.
--     C  c path       `--backend=c --emit exe` links, the binary RUNS without
--                     hanging, AND its observable equals the value the fixture
--                     DECLARES. Compiling is not running and running is not
--                     answering. A fixture that declares nothing scores
--                     `partial` here however cleanly it runs: it produced an
--                     answer and nothing in the tree says the answer is right.
--     N  native       `--backend=direct --emit exe` links and the binary runs.
--                     ONLY `--backend=direct`: plain `duo compile` FALLS BACK
--                     to C and still prints `ok compile`.
--     W  witnessed    the two backends ran and AGREE on stdout and on exit
--                     status. Under `native_only/` the C backend cannot
--                     compile the program at all and is therefore not an
--                     oracle, so the fixture's own `-- expect: N` is the
--                     witness and the direct binary's exit status must equal
--                     it. Same rule scripts/native_differential.duo uses.
--     H  hosted       the SELF-HOSTED front end accepts the fixture:
--                     `std.compiler.parser.parse_status` — the Duo-written
--                     parser running on the Duo-written lexer — returns 0.
--                     This is the only column that asks a question about the
--                     compiler written in Duo rather than the compiler written
--                     in Zig, which is what "hosted" can mean today.
--
-- A CELL IS AGGREGATED OVER THE ROW'S FIXTURES: every fixture reached it `✓`,
-- some reached it `partial`, none reached it `—`. A row with NO FIXTURE prints
-- `—` across and is named under NO FIXTURE, which is the most valuable line in
-- the report — it is the list of capabilities this repository asserts and has
-- never demonstrated.
--
-- WHAT IS CURATED AND WHAT IS MEASURED. Someone has to say "this file
-- demonstrates recursion"; that judgement is the `rows` table below and it is
-- three columns of plain text you can read and check. Nothing else is
-- asserted. Every ✓ is the exit status or the stdout of a command run against
-- that file on this machine, and a listed fixture that does not exist is EXIT
-- 3 rather than a quietly shorter row.
--
-- POSITIVE CONTROLS ARE MANDATORY AND BUILT IN, because a matrix like this
-- fails by reporting a confident all-✓, not by reporting something wrong.
-- Four controls run before the table is printed, and the table is not printed
-- if any of them does not fire:
--
--     syntax   a copy of a fixture with unparseable text appended — loses P
--     type     a copy with a type error appended     — keeps P, loses S
--     value    a copy whose `-- expect:` is falsified — drops C from matched
--              to merely-ran. Its baseline is checked first: the intact
--              fixture must MATCH its own declared value, or falsifying it
--              proves nothing.
--     hosted   the self-hosted parser must not answer the same for every
--              fixture. A pair comparison was tried here and was wrong; see
--              the comment at the check.
--
-- The value control is the one that matters most: it is the only thing between
-- this report and the class of defect §3 of CLAUDE.md was written about, where
-- a harness reported agreement it had not measured.
--
-- DENY FIXTURES are the fifth, and they are real corpus files rather than
-- copies. A row may name fixtures that MUST BE REJECTED — the diagnostic is
-- the capability. They are reported in their own section, with the LAYER that
-- rejected them, and a deny fixture that is ACCEPTED fails the run. They are
-- also the reason the P and S columns are known to discriminate over real
-- corpus files rather than only over damaged copies.
--
-- MEASURED CONTROL, 2026-08-08. Two corpus fixtures were deliberately broken
-- and the report caught both. `examples/spec100/offside.duo`'s `bump` changed
-- from `n + 10` to `n + 11`: exit 3, "does not match its own `-- expect:`" —
-- it is the value control's baseline, so the report refused to run at all
-- rather than print a table whose C column it could no longer defend.
-- `examples/native_differential/rec4_return.duo` given a `to(str)` concat the
-- direct backend refuses: the `record return` row went N ✓ -> partial and
-- W ✓ -> partial, and nothing else moved. Both were restored.
--
-- HOW THIS RECONCILES WITH `zig build capability-matrix`, which is the
-- neighbouring report and reads `proven 86, checked 316` over 361 programs.
-- Two definitions differ ON PURPOSE and neither is a disagreement:
--
--   * its `native` is "the direct backend produced an object". This N also
--     RUNS the binary, so N here is a subset of native there.
--   * its `proven` is the STRICT differential and refuses `native_only/`
--     outright, because the C backend cannot compile those programs and is not
--     an oracle for them; it names the three fixtures in that gap. This W
--     accepts the fixture's own `-- expect: N` there, which is what the
--     BLOCKING gate `zig build native-differential` accepts. So W is looser
--     than its `proven` on exactly `native_only/` and identical everywhere
--     else. `parser_token_stream.duo` is the visible case: `native, no C path`
--     there, W ✓ here.
--
-- Exit 0 pass · 1 a deny fixture was accepted · 3 the report could not run or a
-- control did not fire.
--
-- Env: DUO, CAPROWS_JOBS, CAPROWS_TIMEOUT, CAPROWS_WORK.

work = std.script.getenv("CAPROWS_WORK", "/tmp/duo-capability-rows")

-- name | graph key (`-` = the schema has no slot for this) | fixtures | deny
--
-- The fixture lists are the curated half and the ONLY curated half. Read them
-- against the files: each named file must actually exercise the row's
-- capability, and a file that stops doing so should be removed rather than
-- have its row re-explained.
rows = {
    { "implicit tail return", "-",
      { "examples/native_differential/canon2_tail_record_return.duo",
        "examples/native_differential/canon6_call_in_record_literal.duo",
        "examples/native_differential/canon7_call_local_then_record.duo",
        "examples/spec100/offside.duo" },
      {} },
    { "record return", "\"kind\":\"table_shape\"",
      { "examples/native_differential/canon1_lambda_record_return.duo",
        "examples/native_differential/canon2_tail_record_return.duo",
        "examples/native_differential/rec4_return.duo",
        "examples/native_differential/rec8_token_return.duo" },
      {} },
    { "recursive calls", "-",
      { "examples/native_differential/p10_recurse.duo",
        "examples/native_differential/s2_mutual.duo" },
      {} },
    { "offside blocks", "-",
      { "examples/spec100/offside.duo" },
      { "examples/compile_fail/offside_misindent.duo",
        "examples/compile_fail/offside_end_column.duo" } },
    { "p100 failure routing", "-",
      { "examples/spec100/pack.duo",
        "examples/spec100/consume.duo" },
      {} },
    -- No fixture, deliberately — gap[059]. The two parsers in this tree
    -- disagree about what `X@rel` MEANS: `lib/std/compiler/parser.duo:1386`
    -- projects `(anchor base name)`, which is Pass 100 §4, while the Zig front
    -- end folds infix `@` into MATMUL and `duo check` exits 0 on
    -- `i64@to(str)` with a tensor deprecation warning before codegen dies on
    -- an undeclared `to`. A fixture has to assert something, and either
    -- reading it asserted would convert that disagreement into a claim. `—`
    -- plus a NO FIXTURE line is what an undecided capability is worth.
    { "anchor semantics", "-", {}, {} },
    -- The graph fact a tokenizer would have to carry is its TOKEN DESCRIPTOR.
    -- Not `enum_shape`, which was the first key here and read `—`: the token
    -- kinds in these fixtures are written `Kind = @{ eof = 0, … }`, a data
    -- descriptor, and the graph lifts an `enum_shape` only for the inline
    -- case-set form. That is a real difference between two spellings of the
    -- same idea, and it belongs in the caseset row rather than being papered
    -- over by widening this key until it matches.
    { "tokenizer", "\"kind\":\"table_shape\"",
      { "examples/native_differential/tokenizer.duo",
        "examples/native_differential/tokenizer_selfscan.duo",
        "examples/native_differential/native_only/lexer_native.duo" },
      {} },
    { "parser kernel", "\"kind\":\"table_shape\"",
      { "examples/native_differential/native_only/parser_native.duo",
        "examples/native_differential/native_only/parser_blocks.duo",
        "examples/native_differential/native_only/parser_token_stream.duo" },
      {} },
    { "inline case-set", "\"kind\":\"enum_shape\"",
      { "examples/spec100/caseset.duo" }, {} },
    { "relation edge (level)", "-", { "examples/spec100/edge.duo" }, {} },
    { "relation slot", "-", { "examples/spec100/slot.duo" }, {} },
    { "level application", "-", { "examples/spec100/level.duo" }, {} },
    { "binding-condition loop", "-", { "examples/spec100/consume.duo" }, {} },
    { "correlated guard chain", "-", { "examples/spec100/guard.duo" }, {} },
    { "glued compound assign", "-", { "examples/spec100/glued.duo" }, {} },
    { "x8 field layout", "\"kind\":\"table_shape\"",
      { "examples/spec100/x8.duo" }, {} },
    { "string interpolation", "-",
      { "examples/spec100/offside.duo",
        "examples/spec100/edge.duo" }, {} },
    -- `-`, not `"kind":"table_shape"`. These three fixtures are POSITIONAL
    -- tables (`t = { 10, 20, 30 }`) and the graph's `table_shape` is lifted
    -- from DESCRIPTORS only: `duo graph` on table_const_index.duo emits two
    -- nodes, `module` and `main`, with `"table_shapes":[]`. There is no schema
    -- slot for an anonymous table, so this is a `?` and not a `—`.
    { "native table storage", "-",
      { "examples/native_differential/native_only/table_const_index.duo",
        "examples/native_differential/native_only/table_dynamic_index.duo",
        "examples/native_differential/native_only/table_mutable_store.duo" },
      {} }
}

-- Every unique fixture, and every row's membership, are derived from `rows`.
--
-- The four tables that hold them are LOCAL TO `main` and threaded through
-- `remember` as arguments. They were module-scope `x = {}` bindings, which is
-- the `mwrapper` deny row (`audit100` cannot tell a mutable accumulator from an
-- `M = {}` module wrapper), and nothing outside `main` ever read them — the
-- module scope was reach this file never used.
nofixture = ""
denyrows = ""
denyfail = 0
denytotal = 0

-- One newline-terminated block from a table of lines.
--
-- NOT `lines:join("\n")`, which is the canonical spelling and MISCOMPILES here:
-- in a `str`-returning function it emits `lua_invoke(__fn, 2, duo_args)` with
-- neither identifier declared, and the C compiler rejects the whole program.
-- Before that it silently produced the string `nil`, so the shell probe was a
-- three-byte file and the sweep reported 0 records for 32 fixtures. Worth
-- keeping the note: the failure mode was a WRITTEN nil, not a crash.
weave(lines: any): str
    out = ""
    for i, line in lines
        out = out .. line
        out = out .. "\n"
    end
    out
end

remember(files: any, seen: any, path: str): void
    if seen[path] == nil
        seen[path] = 1
        files[#files + 1] = path
    end
end

-- A path flattened into a filename, so two fixtures with the same basename in
-- different directories cannot share a work file. `parser_native.duo` and a
-- future `native_only/parser_native.duo` would otherwise overwrite each other's
-- graph JSON and the row would read whichever finished last.
slot(path: str): str
    out = ""
    i: i64 = 1
    n: i64 = #path
    while i <= n
        b = path:byte(i)
        if b == 47 or b == 46
            out = out .. "_"
        else
            out = out .. path:sub(i, i)
        end
        i = i + 1
    end
    out
end

-- The per-fixture probe, emitted as shell for the same reason
-- scripts/capability_matrix.duo emits one: measuring a fixture is six or seven
-- process spawns and Duo has no process pool. Duo still does every bit of the
-- REASONING — this writes seven digits and a path and decides nothing.
--
-- EVERY STATUS IS READ WITHOUT A PIPE. `cmd | tail; echo $?` reads tail, and
-- that exact shape produced a false green in this session: `duo compile
-- --backend=direct` piped to `tail` reported 0 for a program the direct
-- backend had REFUSED, because the status belonged to tail. Unpiped, the same
-- command reports 1.
--
-- `DUO_SEMANTIC_CACHE=0` is load-bearing under -P: `duo check` writes one
-- semantic cache file relative to the working directory and parallel probes
-- corrupt each other's reads. capability_matrix.duo measured this — two
-- sweeps minutes apart disagreed on six programs, in the direction that
-- understates the compiler.
--
-- ONE LINE PER TABLE ELEMENT, joined — not a `..` ladder. Two reasons and both
-- matter. The shell is READABLE this way, which is the point of writing it out
-- at all; and `..` beside a string literal is a CLAUDE.md §1 deny row that
-- `audit100` ratchets, so a 40-rung concatenation ladder in a `scripts/` file
-- costs the repo 97 deny hits for nothing. Measured: the ladder form of this
-- one function was the whole of this file's concatlit contribution.
probetext(duo: str, tprefix: str): str
    lines = {
        "f=\"$1\"",
        "s=\"$2\"",
        "d={duo}",
        "t={work}/w/$s",
        "export DUO_SEMANTIC_CACHE=0",
        "des=0",
        "head -1 \"$f\" | grep -q '^--' && des=1",
        -- fmt rewrites in place and deletes comments; the copy takes it.
        "cp \"$f\" $t.fmt.duo",
        "par=0",
        "if $d fmt $t.fmt.duo >/dev/null 2>&1; then par=1; fi",
        "chk=0",
        "if $d check \"$f\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then chk=1; fi",
        "gra=0",
        "if $d graph \"$f\" > {work}/g/$s.json 2>/dev/null; then",
        "  if [ -s {work}/g/$s.json ]; then gra=1; fi",
        "fi",
        -- The declared answer, and which observable it is declared over.
        "want=$(sed -n 's/^-- expect: *//p' \"$f\" | head -1)",
        "decl=0",
        "if [ -n \"$want\" ]; then decl=1; fi",
        "mode=out",
        "case \"$f\" in */native_only/*) mode=exit;; esac",
        -- cst: 0 no C answer · 1 ran, nothing declared · 2 ran and matched.
        "cst=0",
        "crc=-1",
        "if $d compile \"$f\" --backend=c --emit exe -o $t.cb >/dev/null 2>&1; then",
        " if [ -x $t.cb ]; then",
        "  {tprefix}$t.cb > $t.co 2>/dev/null; crc=$?",
        "  if [ $crc -ne 124 ]; then cst=1",
        "   if [ $decl -eq 1 ]; then",
        "    if [ \"$mode\" = exit ]; then got=\"$crc\"",
        "    else got=$(tr '\\n' ' ' < $t.co | sed 's/ *$//'); fi",
        "    if [ \"$got\" = \"$want\" ]; then cst=2; fi",
        "   fi",
        "  fi",
        " fi",
        "fi",
        "nat=0",
        "nrc=-1",
        "if $d compile \"$f\" --backend=direct --emit exe -o $t.db >/dev/null 2>&1; then",
        " if [ -x $t.db ]; then",
        "  {tprefix}$t.db > $t.do 2>/dev/null; nrc=$?",
        "  if [ $nrc -ne 124 ]; then nat=1; fi",
        " fi",
        "fi",
        -- The witness. Under native_only the C backend is not an oracle and
        -- the fixture's own declared answer is; everywhere else it is
        -- agreement, on BOTH stdout and status, because a backend that returns
        -- the right code and prints the wrong text has still miscompiled.
        "wit=0",
        "if [ $nat -eq 1 ]; then",
        " if [ \"$mode\" = exit ]; then",
        "  if [ $decl -eq 1 ] && [ \"$nrc\" = \"$want\" ]; then wit=1; fi",
        " else",
        "  if [ $cst -ge 1 ] && [ $crc -eq $nrc ] && cmp -s $t.co $t.do; then wit=1; fi",
        " fi",
        "fi",
        "printf \"%s%s%s%s%s%s%s\\t%s\\n\" $des $par $chk $gra $cst $nat $wit \"$f\"",
        "rm -f $t.cb $t.db $t.co $t.do $t.fmt.duo"
    }
    weave(lines)
end

-- The self-hosted front end, driven over a list file so the Duo-written parser
-- is loaded once rather than once per fixture. `global P = std.compiler.parser`
-- and the manual line walk are both copied from
-- scripts/parser_corpus_coverage.duo, which is the only other caller and which
-- documents why the walk is by hand.
--
-- THE GENERATED SOURCE CONTAINS NO `{`. It is a Duo string literal in a Duo
-- file, so a `{st}` written for the generated program is interpolated by THIS
-- program instead — the first version emitted `print("" .. st .. "\t" ..
-- path)` with `st` and `path` resolved here, where neither exists, and the C
-- compiler reported two undeclared identifiers inside a 2000-column literal.
-- Joining with `..` in the generated text keeps the two levels apart.
hosttext(): str
    lines = {
        "global P = std.compiler.parser",
        "",
        "main(): i64",
        "    listing = std.script.fs_read(std.script.getenv(\"CAPROWS_LIST\", \"\"))",
        "    n: i64 = #listing",
        "    start: i64 = 1",
        "    i: i64 = 1",
        "    while i <= n + 1",
        "        cut = i > n",
        "        if not cut",
        "            cut = listing:byte(i) == 10",
        "        end",
        "        if cut",
        "            if i > start",
        "                path = listing:sub(start, i - 1)",
        "                f = io.open(path, \"r\")",
        "                if f",
        "                    src = f:read(\"a\")",
        "                    f:close()",
        "                    st = P.parse_status(src)",
        "                    print(to(str)(st) .. \"\\t\" .. path)",
        "                end",
        "            end",
        "            start = i + 1",
        "        end",
        "        i = i + 1",
        "    end",
        "    0",
        "end"
    }
    weave(lines)
end

digit(rec: str, at: i64): i64
    to(i64)(rec:sub(at, at))
end

tab(rec: str): i64
    i: i64 = 1
    n: i64 = #rec
    while i <= n and rec:byte(i) != 9
        i = i + 1
    end
    i
end

-- Display-width padding. Every cell is seven columns wide, and every cell text
-- is ASCII or one of four fixed glyphs, so the pad is chosen by the glyph
-- rather than computed from a byte length that would be wrong for `✓` and `—`.
cellof(reach: i64, total: i64, gap: bool): str
    if gap
        return "   ?   "
    end
    if total == 0
        return "   —   "
    end
    if reach == total
        return "   ✓   "
    end
    if reach == 0
        return "   —   "
    end
    "partial"
end

pad(s: str, w: i64): str
    out = s
    i: i64 = #s
    while i < w
        out = out .. " "
        i = i + 1
    end
    out
end

main = (): i64
    files = {}
    seen = {}
    probe = {}
    host = {}
    duo = std.script.getenv("DUO", "./zig-out/bin/duo")
    jobs = std.script.getenv("CAPROWS_JOBS", "8")
    limit = std.script.getenv("CAPROWS_TIMEOUT", "20")

    if not std.script.fs_exists(duo)
        print("capability_rows: FAIL — no compiler at {duo}; run `zig build`")
        return 3
    end
    -- A matrix measured with yesterday's binary is a confident report about a
    -- build nobody has. capability_matrix.duo caught a stale binary twice while
    -- it was being written; this is the same refusal, not a nested `zig build`,
    -- which would contend for the build cache lock.
    stale = std.script.chomp(std.script.capture(
        "find src -name \"*.zig\" -newer {duo} 2>/dev/null | head -3"))
    if stale != ""
        print("capability_rows: FAIL — {duo} is older than src/; run `zig build`")
        print("  newer than the binary: {stale}")
        return 3
    end

    tprefix = ""
    for i, cand in {"timeout", "gtimeout"}
        if tprefix == "" and std.script.chomp(std.script.capture(
            "command -v {cand} 2>/dev/null")) != ""
            tprefix = "{cand} {limit} "
        end
    end

    std.script.run("rm -rf {work} && mkdir -p {work}/w {work}/g {work}/c")

    -- Collect fixtures, and REFUSE a row that names a file that is not there.
    -- A missing fixture must not read as a shorter row; that is how a
    -- capability quietly stops being measured.
    --
    -- Indexed interpolation is intentionally used below so this report cannot
    -- quietly regress to printing `{r[1]}` as literal text.
    for ri, r in rows
        for fi, f in r[3]
            if not std.script.fs_exists(f)
                print("capability_rows: FAIL — row '{r[1]}' names a missing fixture {f}")
                return 3
            end
            remember(files, seen, f)
        end
        for fi, f in r[4]
            if not std.script.fs_exists(f)
                print("capability_rows: FAIL — row '{r[1]}' names a missing deny fixture {f}")
                return 3
            end
            remember(files, seen, f)
        end
    end
    if #files == 0
        print("capability_rows: FAIL — no fixtures at all; the report is not wired")
        return 3
    end

    -- THE THREE CONTROLS. Built from the corpus so they exercise the same
    -- probe, damaged in three different places so they fail three different
    -- columns. `ctlvalue` is derived from a fixture that DECLARES a value —
    -- without one there is nothing to falsify and the control would be vacuous.
    good = "examples/spec100/offside.duo"
    if not std.script.fs_exists(good)
        print("capability_rows: FAIL — the control source {good} is gone")
        return 3
    end
    ctlsyntax = "{work}/c/ctlsyntax.duo"
    ctltype = "{work}/c/ctltype.duo"
    ctlvalue = "{work}/c/ctlvalue.duo"
    std.script.run("cp {good} {ctlsyntax} && printf 'if if if\\n' >> {ctlsyntax}")
    std.script.run("cp {good} {ctltype} && printf 'bad: i64 = \"notanumber\"\\n' >> {ctltype}")
    std.script.run("sed 's/^-- expect: .*/-- expect: 99 99 99/' {good} > {ctlvalue}")
    remember(files, seen, ctlsyntax)
    remember(files, seen, ctltype)
    remember(files, seen, ctlvalue)

    listpath = "{work}/files.list"
    listing = ""
    for i, f in files
        listing = listing .. f .. "\n"
    end
    std.script.fs_write(listpath, listing)

    std.script.fs_write("{work}/probe.sh", probetext(duo, tprefix))
    -- `xargs -n 2` so each worker gets the path AND its work slot; the slot is
    -- computed here rather than in shell because the shell form would need a
    -- `tr` per fixture and this one is exact.
    joined = ""
    for i, f in files
        joined = joined .. f .. "\n" .. slot(f) .. "\n"
    end
    std.script.fs_write("{work}/pairs.list", joined)
    std.script.run(
        "xargs -P {jobs} -n 2 sh {work}/probe.sh < {work}/pairs.list > {work}/records.tsv 2>/dev/null")

    nrec = 0
    for i, line in std.script.fs_read("{work}/records.tsv"):split("\n")
        rec = std.script.chomp(line)
        if #rec > 8
            t = tab(rec)
            probe[rec:sub(t + 1, #rec)] = rec:sub(1, t - 1)
            nrec = nrec + 1
        end
    end
    if nrec != #files
        got = nrec:to(str)
        want = to(str)(#files)
        print("capability_rows: FAIL — {got} records for {want} fixtures; the sweep lost files")
        return 3
    end

    -- The hosted column. The self-hosted front end is compiled once; a rebuild
    -- costs about eight seconds and doing it per fixture would dominate the run.
    hostsrc = "{work}/hostprobe.duo"
    hostbin = "{work}/hostprobe.bin"
    std.script.fs_write(hostsrc, hosttext())
    if std.script.status("{duo} compile {hostsrc} --emit exe -o {hostbin} >/dev/null 2>&1") != 0
        print("capability_rows: FAIL — the self-hosted front end probe did not build;")
        print("  H cannot be measured, and an unmeasured column must not print as `—`")
        return 3
    end
    std.script.env_set("CAPROWS_LIST", listpath)
    std.script.run("CAPROWS_LIST={listpath} {hostbin} > {work}/host.tsv 2>/dev/null")
    nhost = 0
    for i, line in std.script.fs_read("{work}/host.tsv"):split("\n")
        rec = std.script.chomp(line)
        if #rec > 2
            t = tab(rec)
            host[rec:sub(t + 1, #rec)] = rec:sub(1, t - 1)
            nhost = nhost + 1
        end
    end
    if nhost != #files
        got = nhost:to(str)
        want = to(str)(#files)
        print("capability_rows: FAIL — hosted probe answered {got} of {want}; H is not measured")
        return 3
    end

    -- CONTROLS FIRST, table second. A report that prints its matrix before
    -- checking that it can detect a failure has already told the reader
    -- something it has not earned.
    cs = probe[ctlsyntax]
    ct = probe[ctltype]
    cv = probe[ctlvalue]
    gb = probe[good]
    if digit(cs, 2) != 0
        print("capability_rows: FAIL — the syntax control still PARSES; the P probe is broken")
        return 3
    end
    if digit(ct, 2) != 1 or digit(ct, 3) != 0
        print("capability_rows: FAIL — the type control did not fail sema alone; the S probe is broken")
        print("  wanted parse 1 check 0, got record {ct}")
        return 3
    end
    if digit(gb, 5) != 2
        print("capability_rows: FAIL — {good} does not match its own `-- expect:`;")
        print("  the value control cannot mean anything until it does. record {gb}")
        return 3
    end
    if digit(cv, 5) != 1
        print("capability_rows: FAIL — the value control still MATCHES a falsified")
        print("  `-- expect:`; the C column is not checking values. record {cv}")
        return 3
    end
    -- H's control is that the column is NOT CONSTANT, not that a chosen pair
    -- differs. The pair form was tried and was wrong: it compared the syntax
    -- control against examples/spec100/offside.duo, which the self-hosted
    -- parser ALREADY rejects (it has not learned offside layout), so two
    -- correct rejections read as a broken probe. A column that answers the
    -- same thing for everything is the failure worth catching, and this
    -- catches it whatever the fixture list becomes.
    hostyes = 0
    hostno = 0
    for i, f in files
        if host[f] == "0"
            hostyes = hostyes + 1
        else
            hostno = hostno + 1
        end
    end
    if hostyes == 0 or hostno == 0
        hy = hostyes:to(str)
        hn = hostno:to(str)
        print("capability_rows: FAIL — the hosted probe answered the same for every")
        print("  fixture ({hy} accepted, {hn} rejected); H is not discriminating")
        return 3
    end

    print("")
    print("  Pass 100 §22 — capability matrix, PER CAPABILITY, generated from fixtures")
    print("")
    -- The header is built from the same 7-wide, 1-space cell geometry as the
    -- body rather than typed as one literal, so a column cannot drift out from
    -- under its heading when a cell glyph changes width.
    head = "  " .. pad("capability", 24)
    rule = "  " .. pad("----------", 24)
    for i, cn in {"D", "P", "S", "G", "C", "N", "W", "H"}
        head = head .. " " .. pad("   " .. cn, 7)
        rule = rule .. " " .. "-------"
    end
    print(head)
    print(rule)

    for ri, r in rows
        nm = r[1]
        gk = r[2]
        fs = r[3]
        nf = #fs
        -- A row with no fixture has nothing to say about the graph either. `?`
        -- means "measured, and the schema has no slot"; `—` means "nothing was
        -- measured". Printing `?` for an unfixtured row would claim the first.
        gapg = gk == "-" and nf > 0
        d = 0
        p = 0
        s = 0
        g = 0
        c = 0
        n = 0
        w = 0
        h = 0
        for fi, f in fs
            rec = probe[f]
            if digit(rec, 1) == 1
                d = d + 1
            end
            if digit(rec, 2) == 1
                p = p + 1
            end
            if digit(rec, 3) == 1
                s = s + 1
            end
            -- G asks two things and both must hold: the graph ran, and the
            -- key it would have to carry is in what it emitted.
            if digit(rec, 4) == 1 and std.script.status(
                "grep -qF '{gk}' {work}/g/" .. slot(f) .. ".json 2>/dev/null") == 0
                g = g + 1
            end
            if digit(rec, 5) == 2
                c = c + 1
            end
            if digit(rec, 6) == 1
                n = n + 1
            end
            if digit(rec, 7) == 1
                w = w + 1
            end
            if host[f] == "0"
                h = h + 1
            end
        end
        -- C is three-valued at the fixture too: matched a declared value, or
        -- merely ran. `partial` at the row can therefore mean "some fixtures
        -- matched" or "every fixture ran and none declared anything", and the
        -- C NOTES section below says which.
        cran = 0
        for fi, f in fs
            if digit(probe[f], 5) >= 1
                cran = cran + 1
            end
        end
        ccell = cellof(c, nf, false)
        if nf > 0 and c < nf and cran > c
            ccell = "partial"
        end
        print("  " .. pad(nm, 24) .. " "
            .. cellof(d, nf, false) .. " "
            .. cellof(p, nf, false) .. " "
            .. cellof(s, nf, false) .. " "
            .. cellof(g, nf, gapg) .. " "
            .. ccell .. " "
            .. cellof(n, nf, false) .. " "
            .. cellof(w, nf, false) .. " "
            .. cellof(h, nf, false))
        if nf == 0
            nofixture = nofixture .. "    {nm}\n"
        end

        -- A deny fixture is rejected when SEMA rejects it, and the row also
        -- records whether the PARSER did, because those are different claims
        -- about a diagnostic: an offside mis-indent that only sema catches has
        -- been caught by the wrong layer.
        for fi, f in r[4]
            denytotal = denytotal + 1
            rec = probe[f]
            at = "rejected at sema "
            if digit(rec, 2) == 0
                at = "rejected at parse"
            end
            if digit(rec, 3) == 0
                denyrows = denyrows .. "    {at}  {nm}  {f}\n"
            else
                denyrows = denyrows .. "    ACCEPTED         {nm}  {f}  <- the diagnostic is gone\n"
                denyfail = denyfail + 1
            end
        end
    end

    print("")
    print("  D described (first line states intent) · P parsed (`duo fmt` on a copy)")
    print("  S `duo check` · G `duo graph` names the capability · C C backend ran AND")
    print("  matched its declared value · N `--backend=direct` ran · W both backends")
    print("  agree (or native_only matched `-- expect:`) · H `std.compiler.parser`")
    print("  accepts it — the Duo-written front end, not the Zig one")
    print("")

    if nofixture != ""
        print("  NO FIXTURE — asserted somewhere, demonstrated nowhere")
        io.write(nofixture)
        print("")
    end

    if denytotal > 0
        print("  DENY FIXTURES — the diagnostic IS the capability; these must be rejected")
        io.write(denyrows)
        print("")
    end

    -- The G column's repo-wide caveat, measured rather than remembered. The
    -- pipeline stage these columns come from is "relations / trie / worlds /
    -- demand / witnesses"; the exported graph is nodes only.
    edges = 0
    for i, f in files
        if std.script.status(
            "grep -qF '\"edges\"' {work}/g/" .. slot(f) .. ".json 2>/dev/null") == 0
            edges = edges + 1
        end
    end
    ec = edges:to(str)
    fc = to(str)(#files)
    print("  G NOTES — `duo graph` emits an `edges` array for {ec} of {fc} fixtures.")
    print("  A `?` cell means the graph schema has no slot for that capability at all")
    print("  (`-` in the row's key), so no run of this report could turn it into a ✓.")
    print("  Graph keys, per row:")
    for ri, r in rows
        if #r[3] > 0
            print("    " .. pad(r[1], 24) .. "  " .. r[2])
        end
    end
    print("")

    hy = hostyes:to(str)
    hn = hostno:to(str)
    print("  CONTROLS — all four fired, so a failure in this matrix would show")
    print("    syntax  {ctlsyntax}  lost P")
    print("    type    {ctltype}  kept P, lost S")
    print("    value   {ctlvalue}  fell from matched to merely-ran")
    print("    hosted  the self-hosted parser accepted {hy} and rejected {hn}; not constant")
    print("")

    if denyfail > 0
        df = denyfail:to(str)
        print("capability_rows: FAIL — {df} deny fixture(s) were ACCEPTED")
        return 1
    end
    nr = to(str)(#rows)
    nfx = to(str)(#files)
    print("capability_rows: PASS ({nr} capabilities over {nfx} fixtures)")
    0
end
